    "domain": "Care Transitions",
    "role": "Nurse Care Manager",
    "implementation_guidance": "This implementation guidance leverages the Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research (CFIR) to provide a structured approach for integrating the Structured Care Transitions Protocol into clinical practice. The CFIR's five major domains\u2014Innovation, Outer Setting, Inner Setting, Characteristics of Individuals, and Process\u2014offer a comprehensive lens through which to plan, execute, and evaluate implementation efforts. 1. Assess organizational readiness by evaluating the innovation's evidence base, relative advantage, adaptability, and cost. 2. Evaluate existing infrastructure, including IT systems, work infrastructure, and relational connections. 3. Identify key stakeholders, including Nurse Care Managers, patients, and families, and assess their knowledge, beliefs, and self-efficacy regarding the protocol. 4. Develop a detailed implementation plan that includes specific goals, timelines, and resource allocation. 5. Engage stakeholders throughout the process to foster ownership and identify champions. 6. Execute the plan by training staff, integrating the protocol into workflows, and providing ongoing support. 7. Reflect on and evaluate implementation progress and outcomes, collecting data on process metrics, patient outcomes, and implementation fidelity. 8. Adapt the protocol and implementation strategies based on evaluation findings and evolving contextual factors.",
    "expected_outcomes": "Enhanced outcomes with specific metrics and confidence intervals include: a 22% reduction in 30-day hospital readmission rates (RR = 0.78; 95% CI: 0.70\u20130.87), an 18% reduction in 90-day hospital readmission rates (RR = 0.82; 95% CI: 0.74\u20130.91), a 25% reduction in 30-day ED visit rates (RR = 0.75; 95% CI: 0.66\u20130.85), a 20% reduction in 90-day ED visit rates (RR = 0.80; 95% CI: 0.71\u20130.90), and significant improvements in Quality of Life (SMD = 0.45; 95% CI: 0.32\u20130.58) and patient satisfaction (SMD = 0.52; 95% CI: 0.38\u20130.66).",
    "target_population": "The target population includes all adult patients undergoing care transitions, with particular attention to those at high risk for readmission or adverse outcomes due to complex medical conditions, polypharmacy, cognitive impairment, or socioeconomic vulnerabilities. This includes individuals from racial and ethnic minority groups, those of low socioeconomic status, those in rural and geographically isolated areas, individuals with limited health literacy, older adults, individuals with disabilities, and patients with chronic conditions and multiple comorbidities.",
    "evidence": "The Structured Care Transitions Protocol is supported by a robust body of evidence. A systematic review and meta-analysis by Sakashita et al. (2025) found that nurse-led transitional care interventions significantly reduce 30-day hospital readmission rates (RR = 0.78; 95% CI: 0.70\u20130.87) and 90-day hospital readmission rates (RR = 0.82; 95% CI: 0.74\u20130.91). The same study also reported significant reductions in 30-day ED visit rates (RR = 0.75; 95% CI: 0.66\u20130.85) and 90-day ED visit rates (RR = 0.80; 95% CI: 0.71\u20130.90). Furthermore, the meta-analysis demonstrated significant improvements in Quality of Life (SMD = 0.45; 95% CI: 0.32\u20130.58) and patient satisfaction (SMD = 0.52; 95% CI: 0.38\u20130.66). Implementation science research, guided by frameworks like the CFIR, helps to tailor interventions, optimize strategies, and ensure the sustainability and scalability of effective care transition programs.",
